  "notes": [
    "All I/O is 3.3 V and not 5 V tolerant. Bank 1 (the pins from 20 down to 6 on the right, plus SS, SCK, SI and SO) is fixed at 3.3 V because the flash and FT232H share it. Banks 0 and 2 can be moved to another voltage through the VIO pin and solder jumpers.",
    "Header labels are the FPGA's SG48 package pin numbers, which is what goes in the PCF file. Pin 23 on the silkscreen is set_io 23.",
    "The 12 MHz oscillator always reaches the 12M header pin. It only reaches the FPGA (pin 20, a global input) once you bridge the solder jumper marked OSC. The vendor PCF calls it R16, the v3.1 schematic SJ16.",
    "The FT232H is wired as the SPI programmer for the flash, and optionally for the FPGA's configuration RAM. The official PCF also names pins 14 and 15 as a serial link to it, and both are shared with the flash.",
    "The FPGA boots from a 4 MB SPI flash on SS, SCK, SI and SO, so those four pins are busy at power-up and whenever you program. qSPI mode needs two solder jumpers and costs pins 10 and 20.",
    "The RGB LED pins (R, G, B) are open drain only. Pin 35 is the vendor's recommended clock input because the PLL sits next to it.",
    "A 2-pin header at the far end from USB repeats 5V and GND.",
    "UPduino v3.0 and v3.1 have identical header wiring. The one difference worth knowing: v3.0 boards have GND and 12M swapped on the silkscreen (right side, rows 7 and 8). This page follows the v3.1 schematic and silkscreen, which agree.",
    "Needs checking: the vendor pinout image, the readthedocs pinout table and the MPSSE section of the official PCF put FPGA pin 17 on SI and pin 14 on SO. The v3.1 schematic, with its default resistors, puts pin 14 on SI and pin 17 on SO, which is the only order that lets the FPGA boot from flash. This page follows the schematic.",
    "Needs checking: the schematic's RGB LED symbol names its pins in a different color order from the net names. This page follows the net names, the PCF and the silkscreen: red on pin 41, green on 39, blue on 40."
  ],
